# Force LF for files whose bytes are hashed, executed as scripts, or compared byte-for-byte by a
# test, so behavior is identical regardless of the checking-out OS/git config. Without this, a
# Windows clone (core.autocrlf=true) and a Linux CI runner can end up with different line endings
# for the "same" file, which would break MigrationImmutabilityTest's checksums (KH-0.2.2), shell
# script shebangs, and ErrorCodesDocGenerationTest's byte-for-byte comparison of error-codes.md
# (KH-0.6a — surfaces on Windows CRLF checkouts).
*.sql text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.lock text eol=lf
# KH-0.3 Phase-0 closure: pin Java sources to LF too. Git already stores them as LF blobs, but a
# Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true checks them out as CRLF; `docker build` then COPYs that
# CRLF into the container, where Spotless (google-java-format) rejects every file. Same class of
# OS-independence problem the lines above solve — and a prerequisite for scripts/smoke.sh to "work
# locally on Windows Git-Bash and in CI" (KH-0.3 Part 3). No-op on Linux CI (already LF).
*.java text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
# KH-0.3.2: a trailing \r on a Vuln ID would silently break .trivyignore matching on a
# Windows CRLF checkout — pin it the same way the SQL/checksum/shell files are pinned.
.trivyignore text eol=lf
docs/error-codes.md text eol=lf
docs/api/openapi.json text eol=lf
